\description{
The backend-neutral layer between the encodings and a storage format.
Encodings (\verb{R/io-*.R}) never call rhdf5 directly, they call the functions
here, which dispatch on the class of the handle they are given to a backend
(\code{store_backend()}).
HDF5 (\code{h5_backend()}) and zarr (\code{zarr_backend()}, which writes v3 only so
far) each have a list of the same operations.
}
\details{
A \code{store} is an open file or group, and a \code{node} is an open group or array.
Both are handles of the backend, and paths are absolute within the store.

A \code{selection} is \code{NULL} (the whole array) or a list with one entry per
dimension, each \code{NULL} (every index) or a strictly increasing vector of
1-based indices.
Dimensions are in Python's order, so \code{selection = list(1:2, NULL)} is numpy's
\verb{x[0:2, :]}.

The array information from \code{array_info()} is a one-row data frame, as
\code{h5ls()} gives, with \code{name}, \code{dclass} (\code{INTEGER}, \code{FLOAT}, \code{ENUM},
\code{COMPOUND} or \code{STRING}), \code{dtype} and \code{rank}, and the list columns \code{shape}
and \code{chunks} (numeric vectors in Python's order).
\code{chunks} is \code{NULL} for a contiguous HDF5 dataset and for a 0-d array, and
